In this paper, we present a novel domain adaptation framework for medical image segmentation, which addresses the problem of segmenting images from unseen domains. Our approach leverages a combination of domain-invariant feature learning and adversarial training to improve the generalization ability of deep neural networks to new domains. We demonstrate the effectiveness of our method on several benchmark datasets, including lung tumor segmentation, brain structure segmentation, and retinal vessel segmentation. Our experiments show that our proposed method outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods in terms of segmentation accuracy, especially when dealing with large domain shifts. Additionally, we perform an ablation study to analyze the contribution of each component of our framework, providing insights into the working mechanism of our approach. The main contributions of this work can be summarized as follows:

* A novel domain adaptation framework for medical image segmentation that combines domain-invariant feature learning and adversarial training;
* Experimental validation of the proposed method on several publicly available datasets, demonstrating improved performance compared to state-of-the-art methods, especially when facing large domain shifts;
* An ablation study to investigate the effectiveness of different components of the proposed framework, providing valuable insights into its working mechanism.

Overall, our work has important implications for improving the robustness of deep learning models for medical image segmentation tasks, where domain shift is a common challenge due to variations in imaging protocols, patients, or diseases.

This paper proposes a novel approach for polarimetric synthetic aperture radar (SAR) image semantic segmentation, which combines the advantages of 3D discrete wavelet transform (DWT) and Markov random field (MRF). The proposed method exploits the spatial and spectral information in polarimetric SAR images by using 3D DWT to decompose the data into different frequency bands. Then, MRF is employed to model the dependencies between adjacent pixels in each band, enabling the integration of both local and global contextual knowledge. The proposed framework is evaluated on several datasets collected from different platforms and scenarios, demonstrating its superiority over existing methods in terms of accuracy and computational efficiency. Specifically, our method achieves an average overall accuracy of 90.5% and 86.7% for two publicly available datasets, outperforming other state-of-the-art techniques. Moreover, we show that incorporating 3D DWT and MRF components improves the baseline method by 10.4% and 20%, respectively. Additionally, experimental results demonstrate that our method exhibits robustness against various environmental conditions such as weather, illumination variations, and sensor noise.

The main contributions of this paper can be summarized as follows:

* A novel Polarimetric SAR image semantic segmentation method combining 3D Discrete Wavelet Transform and Markov Random Fields;
* Exploration of the complementary information provided by polarimetric SAR data through the use of 3D DWT;
* Integration of local and global contextual knowledge utilizing MRFs;
* Demonstration of improved performance compared to state-of-the-art methods on multiple datasets under varying environmental conditions;
* Quantitative analysis highlighting the effectiveness of the individual components of the proposed framework.

